  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA low-amplitude ridge-west/trof-east pattern persists. This pattern \ndoes get interrupted this weekend, as an upper-level shortwave \nridge tries to push into the Great Lakes. But this ridge \ngradually deamplifies and melts away, returning us to our \npreviously scheduled wnw-flow programming. Areas of more active\nwx will continue to be found to our north, and to our south, \nfor the next few days.\n\nNo precip or synoptic-scale cloud cover early this AM. However, \npatchy fog and low clouds are developing as expected, especially\nin the south (where the BL is more moist). That will gradually \nburn off as we move thru mid- morning. Our highest-progged \ninstability by afternoon extends from MBL/CAD- ne-ward into the \nStraits. MlCape values reach up to 500j/kg here, aided by \nmoisture from Lake MI. With very light synoptic winds today, the\nstrongest forcing today will be in the eastern UP, where lake \nbreezes will collide in the interior in the afternoon. Our \nability to turn instability into convection elsewhere is less \ncertain, with weaker forcing along lake breeze boundaries. And a\nlot of the energy in the broader region will focus along one or\nmore MCSs tracking across the southern lakes this afternoon/ \ntonight. Tonight, some guidance tries to bring convective \nremnants east from the MI/WI border toward the Straits. Chancy \npops in order for much of tonight for eastern upper and far \nnorthern lower MI.\n\nUnfortunately, model spread regarding shortwave impulses tracking \nacross the area mid-week has increased. Convective feedback \ninto the broader synoptic pattern is becoming more substantial\nin model progs (which is likely correct). For Wednesday during\nthe day, it looks like we're out ahead of any even weak \nforcing. So lake-breeze focused convection should be the main \nshow, such as it is. That means a chance for afternoon storms \nin eastern upper and ne lower MI. Can something take a run at us\nfrom the wnw Wed night? Guidance pops are very unimpressed. A \nsmall chance for convection returns south of M-32 Thursday.\n\nSurface high pressure builds se-ward into the northern lakes region \nto close out the work week. This will bring cooler/drier wx for a \nshort while, Thu night to Fri night. But return flow behind the \ndeparting high, and ahead of a surface cold front and some shortwave \nforcing aloft, will transition us wetter for the weekend. \nPotentially a decent shot for what would be needed rain for many, \nbut lots can change between now and then. Somewhat cool/dry \nnortherly low- level flow behind that front for early next week.\nTemps are a little cooler Friday to Monday, with highs in the \n70s, as opposed to near 80f/low 80s the other days of the next \nweek.",
